   $q$How many points do the parabola $y = x^2 - 4x + 2$ and the line $y = 2x - 7$ share?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$1$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$2$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"assumed-two-solutions-from-quadratic"},{"text":"$0$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misread-zero-discriminant-as-none"},{"text":"it cannot be found without a graph","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-graph-required"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$1$$q$, $q$Set $x^2 - 4x + 2 = 2x - 7$, so $x^2 - 6x + 9 = 0$. The discriminant is $36 - 36 = 0$, so there is exactly $1$ solution, $x = 3$, at the point $(3, -1)$. The line is tangent to the parabola. A quadratic does not always have $2$ solutions; a zero discriminant means one.$q$, 'draft', null)
