  ('37221655-5c31-5381-b8ec-7813b242acc9', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'systems-of-equations'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Solve the system $x + y + z = 6$, $2x - y + z = 3$, and $x + 2y - z = 2$:$q$, $q$[{"text":"$(1, 2, 9)$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"sign-error-solving-for-z"},{"text":"$(2, 1, 3)$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"swapped-x-and-y"},{"text":"$(3, 2, 1)$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reversed-order-of-variables"},{"text":"$(1, 2, 3)$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$(1, 2, 3)$$q$, $q$Add the first and third equations: $2x + 3y = 8$. Add the second and third: $3x + y = 5$, so $y = 5 - 3x$. Then $2x + 15 - 9x = 8$ gives $x = 1$, so $y = 2$ and $z = 6 - 1 - 2 = 3$. Check: $2 - 2 + 3 = 3$ and $1 + 4 - 3 = 2$. The answer $(1, 2, 9)$ solves the first equation as $z = 6 + x + y$.$q$, 'draft', null)
