  ('24b96647-211f-50dd-b0dc-de3fbe38ec6b', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'systems-of-equations'), null, 'hard',
   $q$For which radii $r$ does the line $y = x + 4$ cross the circle $x^2 + y^2 = r^2$ at two points?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$r = 2\\sqrt{2}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"gave-tangent-case"},{"text":"$r > 4$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-intercept-as-distance"},{"text":"$r > 2\\sqrt{2}$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$r < 2\\sqrt{2}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reversed-inequality"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$r > 2\sqrt{2}$$q$, $q$Substitute: $x^2 + (x + 4)^2 = r^2$, so $2x^2 + 8x + 16 - r^2 = 0$. Two points need a positive discriminant: $64 - 8(16 - r^2) = 8r^2 - 64 > 0$, so $r^2 > 8$ and $r > 2\sqrt{2}$. At $r = 2\sqrt{2}$ the line is only tangent, with one point.$q$, 'draft', null)
